#
# `hash` with no operand: bash writes a table with a header and a hit count,
# and it is the only shell on the panel that writes anything of the sort.
#
# The four listings are four formats — zsh and ksh93 write `name=/path`, dash
# writes the bare path, bash writes columns under `hits command` — so this is
# a case that could only ever have lived in a dialect file. What is asked is
# the whole listing rather than a status, because the format *is* the answer.
#
# The commands are run first so that the table has something in it, and they
# are run in an order this file chose: a hash table is a table and the order
# it lists in is the shell's, not the order the names were typed.

hash
printf 'nothing hashed yet -> %s\n' "$?"

ls > /dev/null 2>&1
cat /dev/null
hash
printf 'two commands later -> %s\n' "$?"

# A second run of the same command moves the count rather than adding a row.
ls > /dev/null 2>&1
hash

# -r empties it, and the empty listing is a sentence rather than nothing.
hash -r
hash
printf 'after -r -> %s\n' "$?"

# A name given to `hash` is looked up and remembered without running it.
hash cat
hash
